  "query_preview": "..Southwesterly flow aloft resumes across our forecast \narea today as ridging aloft moves to the east with a trough to our \nnorth and west. This setup will shunt most of the mid level moisture \nwe saw Tuesday off to the east closer to the NM border. With that \nsaid, still enough residual moisture aloft for some areas of virga \nmainly along and east of a line from Bisbee-Willcox-Safford-Hannagan \nMeadow. These will be capable of producing gusty outflow winds and \nthe HREF does indicate a 30 to 50 percent chance (40 km neighborhood \nprobability) of wind gusts greater than 30 kts near the NM border \nthis afternoon. Similar to yesterday, we can't rule out a lightning \nstrike or two. Outside of that, we'll have larger scale southwest \nwinds increase to around 15-20 mph this afternoon with gusts up to \n25-30 mph resulting in elevated to locally near critical fire \nweather conditions. Temperatures will lower a few degrees today, but \nstill remain about 5 to 10 degrees above normal. \n\nFor Thursday, the pressure gradients will remain elevated especially \nover southern Cochise County where breezes of 15-20 mph with gusts \nof 25-30 mph are expected with elevated fire weather conditions. \nElsewhere, winds will be a bit lower and temperatures area-wide drop \na few more degrees compared to today. \n\nDry westerly flow for Friday and Saturday with less wind (just \ntypical afternoon breezes), then another dry trough is progged to \nmove through on Sunday and this will result in increased west to \nsouthwest winds. Over the next few days, we'll be monitoring this \nfor potential fire weather concerns. This system will bring in \ncooler temperatures Sunday and Monday with highs dropping to near or \neven slightly below normal levels.",
